<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Taifa of Almería</h1><p class="st-meta">1013 CE – 1091 CE (approx.) · capital Almería</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A wealthy port taifa famous for its silk, whose poet-king al-Mutasim ibn Sumadih made Almería a centre of Andalusi culture.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almeria">Almería</a> had become the main naval base of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/caliphate-of-cordoba">Caliphate of Córdoba</a>. After its fall, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amirids">Amirid</a> freedman Khayran seized the city around 1013, followed by another freedman, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zuhayr-of-almeria">Zuhayr</a>, who was killed in 1038. After a period under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, the Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tujibids">Tujibid</a> family of the Banu Sumadih took power in 1041 under Man ibn Sumadih.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almeria">Almería</a> prospered as the busiest port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>, trading with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a> and producing fine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silks</a> and brocades on thousands of looms. Al-Mutasim (1052-1091) enlarged the great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcazaba-of-almeria">Alcazaba</a> and gathered poets and scholars at his court, while paying tribute to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and quarrelling with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/taifa-of-granada">Taifa of Granada</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Al-Mutasim died in 1091 as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a> besieged the city, and his son fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almeria">Almería</a> became an Almoravid province, briefly broke away as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/second-taifa-of-almeria">Second Taifa of Almería</a> in 1145, and then fell to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> in 1147.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/taifa-of-almeria.jpg" alt="Taifa of Almería" width="320" height="271"><figcaption>Image: Tyk · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Location_map_Taifa_of_Almer%C3%ADa.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1013 CE – 1091 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Almería</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Khayran al-Amiri, Zuhayr al-Amiri, al-Mutasim ibn Sumadih</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Andalusi Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Almería</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1052&amp;focus=taifa-of-almeria" aria-label="Taifa of Almería on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4618 850 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Taifa of Almería lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4938 1106L4881 1071M4938 1106L4799 1289M4938 1106L4958 1078M4938 1106L4972 1068"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4881 1071h0M4799 1289h0M4958 1078h0M4972 1068h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4938 1106h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4938 1106h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Almería. <a href="/atlas?year=1052&amp;focus=taifa-of-almeria">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
